  "rate1_finding": "THE WINDOW SCALES WITH THE ANIMATION; it is not a fixed number of frames. The rifle (38-frame atk1) chains at 16, 20, 24, 28 and 32 and not at 8, 12 or 36. The handgun (29-frame atk1) chains at 16 and 24 and NOT at 28 -- which an absolute window shared with the rifle would have to accept. Each window sits inside its own weapon's attack rather than at a shared offset; the exact numbers are a ROM FIELD (data/player_combo_windows.json), and an earlier gloss of '42%..84% of the animation' is WITHDRAWN -- across four weapons the opening fractions are 0.42, 0.52, 0.33 and 0.38. Bounds are resolved to the gaps actually swept, not to single frames: the rifle opens in (12,16] and closes in [32,36), the handgun opens in (12,16] and closes in [24,28).",
  "weapon_type_poke": {
    "_": "Writing 0x021A217C on the rate-1.0 RIFLE state (its own atk1 = 38) before stepping any frames, then pressing attack. If the byte drove the animation the hold would become the poked type's atk1; it stays 38 every time, so the byte RECORDS the equipped type and the archive is what actually plays. The archive is loaded at equip time, so another weapon's frames are not in memory.",
